{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "avg-08-average-rates-prices-and-productivity",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Average Rates, Prices and Productivity",
    "description": "Average quotients correctly by returning each displayed rate to the real total amount and real total quantity behind it.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with equal quantities where a simple mean happens to work, then deliberately break that shortcut with equal-money purchases. Reverse the rate to kg-per-rupee, convert completion times to productivity rates, use day/attendance frequencies as weights, solve the fixed-plus-variable expense pattern and end with a multi-price petrol capstone. The recurring question is: what are the true total numerator and denominator?",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "Teach why displayed rates often cannot be averaged directly. Cover equal quantities bought at different prices, equal money spent at different prices, kg-per-rupee buying rates, workers with different completion times converted to units per minute, different day types with different visitor counts, charges weighted by attendance ratios, and the fixed-plus-variable boarding-expense pattern from the exercise. End with a petrol-cost capstone where equal money is spent at several prices, so litres purchased become the correct denominator."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the averages that look simple but punish a quick 'add the rates and divide'.\n\nA rate is already a fraction. Before averaging rates, we need to know what was actually totalled on top and what was actually totalled underneath.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Rates are fractions in disguise. Start with an easy case: buy 1 kg at ₹20 per kg and another 1 kg at ₹30 per kg.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Total cost = ₹50\nTotal quantity = 2 kg\nAverage cost = 50 ÷ 2 = ₹25 per kg\n\nEqual quantities give equal weights, so here the simple mean happens to work.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
